  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nA mid-to-upper level shortwave trough ejecting out from the \nnorthern Rockies tomorrow will bring some mid-level vorticity \nand cold air advection to the region, helping to increase \ninstability and buoyancy (2,000-3,500J/kg of MUCAPE) across the \narea. In addition, surface pressure falls ahead of the shortwave\ndisturbance, will help organize an around 1006mb surface \ncyclone across north central Nebraska and central South Dakota. \nAs this cyclone strengthens across the afternoon and evening \nhours, a surface cold front will undergoing frontogenesis across\ncentral Nebraska/Kansas. This feature will likely serve as the \nprimary mechanism for evening thunderstorm development, likely \ndriving a line of convection across a majority of the area from \nnorthwest to southeast between mainly 7PM and 1AM. Given the \nhigh instability (>2,000J/kg of CAPE), steepening low-level \nlapse rates (up to 8-9C/km) and 25-40kts of bulk shear (EBWD); \nthe potential for severe weather will remain. The main concern \nwould be for strong to damaging wind gusts as high as 60-70MPH. \nThough not the primary concern, a few of the stronger storms may\nstill be able to grow tall enough to form hail stones up to the\nsize of quarters. The Storm Prediction Center includes the full\narea in a marginal risk (level 1 of 5) with most of central \nNebraska areas near and north of I-80 included in a slight risk \n(level 2 of 5). \n\nBeyond the chance for precipitation Thursday, winds out of the \nsoutheast may become breezy at times, gusting as high as 20-30MPH \nduring the afternoon hours. Highs, despite the mostly cloudy to \novercast skies will still be expected to raise as high as the upper \n80s and 90s. Following the exit of the storms Thursday night, the \nforecast remains dry through at least Monday (20-50% precipitation \nchance returns Tuesday night). Southwest U.S. ridging across the \nweekend will help fend off most precipitation chances as the jet \nstream is shoved further northward. Breezy conditions will be \nexpected to return Friday afternoon (northerly gusts as high as 25-\n35MPH) as the surface low pull east and away. lighter winds over the \nweekend will come as higher pressure resettles back into region. \nTemperatures behind the front Friday and Saturday should drop back \ndown to the 80s. Warming temperatures in response to clearing skies, \nwill warm temperatures back up to the upper 80s to mid 90s for the \nstart off next week.",
